{"name":"Great Fennel Flower","hex":"#719ba2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"great","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Taking up a lot of space; large.\n\n Much, more than usual.","Much, more than usual."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Expression of gladness and content about something.","A sarcastic inversion thereof."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/great"},{"term":"fennel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant, Foeniculum vulgare, of the parsley family, which has a sweet, anise-like flavor.","The bulb, leaves, or stalks of the plant, eaten as a vegetable."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fennel"},{"term":"flower","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colorful, conspicuous structure associated with angiosperms, frequently scented and attracting various insects, and which may or may not be used for sexual reproduction.","A reproductive structure in angiosperms (flowering plants), often conspicuously colourful and typically including sepals, petals, and either or both stamens and/or a pistil."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To put forth blooms.","To decorate with pictures of flowers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flower"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fennel","blocks":[{"text":"Fennel is a flowering plant species in the carrot family. It is a hardy, perennial herb with yellow flowers and feathery leaves. It is indigenous to the shores of the Mediterranean but has become widely naturalized in many parts of the world, especially on dry soils near the sea coast and on riverbanks."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Foeniculum_July_2011-1a.jpg/330px-Foeniculum_July_2011-1a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fennel","wikidata":"Q43511"},{"title":"Flower","blocks":[{"text":"Flowers, also known as blossoms and blooms, are the reproductive structures of flowering plants. Typically, they are structured in four circular levels around the end of a stalk. These include: sepals, which are modified leaves that support the flower; petals, often designed to attract pollinators; male stamens, where pollen is presented; and female gynoecia, where pollen is received and its movement is facilitated to the egg. When flowers are arranged in a group, they are known collectively as an inflorescence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg/330px-Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower","wikidata":"Q506"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Fenouil.jpg/250px-Fenouil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Fenouil.jpg/500px-Fenouil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Fenouil.jpg/250px-Fenouil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFenouil.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Fennel_flower_heads.jpg/250px-Fennel_flower_heads.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Fennel_flower_heads.jpg/500px-Fennel_flower_heads.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Fennel_flower_heads.jpg/250px-Fennel_flower_heads.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFennel_flower_heads.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wi